{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Texture Paint Face and Image Masking.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Texture Paint Face and Image Masking.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"texture paint face and image masking texture paint face and image masking","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Texture Paint Face and Image Masking.md","fileName":"Texture Paint Face and Image Masking.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Texture Paint Face and Image Masking","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-14 01:18\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Blender Texture Painting Workflow]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Blender Texture Painting Workflow","creatinggameenvironmentsinblender3d.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Texture Paint Face and Image Masking.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Texture Paint Face and Image Masking.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"texture paint face and image masking texture paint face and image masking","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Texture Paint Face and Image Masking.md","fileName":"Texture Paint Face and Image Masking.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Texture Paint Face and Image Masking","heading":"Texture Paint Face and Image Masking","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Face masking restricts painting to selected mesh faces, connecting surface selection with the pixels those faces occupy. Image or stencil masks instead restrict the stroke according to a two-dimensional pattern.\n\nThese mechanisms solve different targeting problems and can be combined. Face selection protects unrelated object regions, while an image mask shapes detail inside the permitted area without requiring every boundary to follow mesh topology.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Blender Texture Painting Workflow","creatinggameenvironmentsinblender3d.pdf"],"properties":[